WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.719
Why is the Moon so big?

00:00:01.720 --> 00:00:03.160
Actually, it’s all in your head.

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:04.060
What?!?!?!

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:07.920
Hey Crazies.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:10.760
Have you ever looked at the Moon when it’s
really low in the sky?

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:14.400
It has that reddish-orange color and it looks
so much bigger.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.580
The color is a real effect caused by the Earth’s
atmosphere.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:20.840
The light has to pass through more air near
the horizon…

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:23.760
and air scatters away the blue half of the
spectrum.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:26.920
The same thing happens with the sun at sunrise
and sunset.

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:29.820
but the hugeness of the Moon is a complete
illusion.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:31.860
No, for real, it's all in your head.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:35.720
It’s called the “Moon Illusion” and
it has fascinated humans for a very long time.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:36.780
To the timeline!

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:40.980
The earliest record we have of the illusion
is from a cuneiform tablet that’s been dated

00:00:40.989 --> 00:00:44.050
to the time of King Assur-bani-pal in Ancient
Mesopotamia.

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:48.670
That’s modern-day Iraq, Syria, and Turkey;
but, like, a long time ago!

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:52.190
We also have a record from Ancient China during
the time of Confucius

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:55.640
Back then though, people were convinced it
was a real effect.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:01:00.379
They thought the moon must be physically closer
or the optics of the atmosphere must enlarge

00:01:00.379 --> 00:01:01.510
the image.

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:04.870
Even Aristotle in Ancient Greece was convinced
it was optical

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:06.920
and we all know how his ideas turned out.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:11.740
It wasn’t until about 1021 CE that someone
finally questioned it.

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:18.120
Abu Ali al-Ḥasan ibn al-Ḥasan ibn al-Haytham
pioneered the concept of experimentation over

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:23.800
600 years before Galileo making him one of
the first actual scientists.

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:27.860
He suggested optics weren’t quite enough
to explain the moon illusion.

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:29.520
Why can’t it be a real thing?

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:32.740
Because the Moon’s distance from us doesn’t
change very much.

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:36.880
We saw in a previous that the Moon is this
far away from the Earth

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:41.450
and because it’s so far away, any changes
in its distance will go mostly unnoticed.

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:45.920
Yes, the Moon’s orbit is elliptical, but
drawing it like this is misleading.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:47.170
It looks more like this.

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:49.080
It’s almost circular.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:53.740
That means, over the course of a month, the
Moon’s distance only changes this much.

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:55.040
That’s not that much.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.800
So anytime the news or your friends are like:

00:01:57.800 --> 00:01:59.400
Oh my god! It’s a super moon!

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.080
It’s actually not that big of a deal.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:03.040
There’s nothing super about it!

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:08.120
To make matters worse, the moon looks bigger
on the horizon, where it’s actually farther away

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:11.540
This is the line you look along when the moon
is high in the sky.

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:14.860
But this is the line you look along when the
moon is near the horizon.

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:17.580
That's an entire Earth radius farther!

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:21.200
That alone easily cancels out anything optical
happening in the atmosphere.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:22.740
So, what’s really going on?

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:25.560
There are actually several different explanations.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:27.980
Psychologists treat them like they’re competing
explanations,

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:31.760
but, for reasons I’ll make clear later,
I think it’s all of them at the same time.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:32.780
Let’s do this!

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:35.940
First, my personal favorite: the Ebbinghaus
Illusion.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:39.960
Your brain actually changes the sizes of things
to add extra contrast.

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:42.200
Take a look at the orange circles in this
picture.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:45.080
The one surrounded by tiny blue circles looks
bigger,

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:49.160
but, if you remove the blue circles, you can
see they’re the same size.

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:53.780
Even now that you know they’re the same
size, you still see them different.

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:58.300
In fact, if I animate one, you see the orange
circle change size even though it doesn’t

00:02:58.300 --> 00:02:59.380
actually change.

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:03.280
The idea is that a similar thing happens to
the Moon when it’s near the horizon.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:07.740
As the Moon rises, you have a bunch of buildings
and trees to compare it to.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:10.260
so you’re brain’s all like “Wow, that’s
really big.”

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:15.220
But, then when it gets higher, it’s surrounded
only by the vast emptiness of the night sky,

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:16.460
so it looks smaller.

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:20.860
If you remove everything else from view by,
say, looking through a paper towel roll

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:22.770
or even just looking at everything upside
down

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:27.280
then the Moon illusion is lessened for some
people and eliminated for others.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:31.180
But, since it’s not eliminated for everyone,
we need more explanation.

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:34.460
The second possible explanation is the Ponzo
Illusion.

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:37.780
This is an example of something we call “size
constancy.”

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:41.700
Basically, our brains know that many things
don’t just randomly change size.

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:46.680
So if we see something like a person get smaller,
our brains just interpret that as though the

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:48.750
person is moving farther away.

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:52.460
The flip-side is that, if something looks
like it’s getting farther away,

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:55.640
but doesn’t change size, then it must be
growing.

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:59.260
So the idea here is, as long as we have the
same kind of receding perspective

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:03.209
with the sky that we do with the ground, it
could happen with the Moon.

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:06.440
When the Moon is near the horizon, it must
be farther away,

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:09.760
but it’s always the same size, so your brain
thinks it’s bigger

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:12.390
and it changes your perception accordingly.

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:17.280
The problem is that very few people think
the horizon Moon looks farther away.

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:19.160
Most people say it looks closer.

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:21.640
Finally, we have the Sky-Dome Model.

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:24.880
Unlike the Ponzo illusion which assumes a
flat sky

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:29.080
the sky-dome model assumes a curved sky above
a flat ground.

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:32.900
It suggests that maybe our brains map all
the really far stuff

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:37.050
like stars and the moon, onto an imaginary
dome above our heads.

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:41.340
The advantage here is that it says we connect
the sky to the ground at the horizon

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:45.300
and, as a result, underestimate the distance
to sky objects.

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:51.160
We saw in the other video that, at best, we
can only see about 3 miles in any direction.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:55.180
That puts the ground horizon in this picture
about 3 miles away,

00:04:55.180 --> 00:05:00.060
so our brains assume the distant clouds in
the sky are also 3 miles away

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:03.100
when those clouds are actually about 10 miles
away.

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:04.660
A huge underestimation.

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:09.640
The thing is though, none of these are dramatic
enough to explain the Moon illusion.

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:12.940
But if we consider all three at the same time,
we might have something.

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:16.120
The Moon illusion is subtle for some and dramatic
for others.

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:18.600
A small fraction of people don’t even see
it.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:21.960
Maybe that’s because not everyone is affected
the same way by these.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:25.440
Someone who is greatly affected a lot by all of these,
like, say, my Mom,

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:29.340
is going to just be mind blown by the size
of the Moon every time.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:31.300
So, how affected are you by the Moon illusion?

